; Lists are values, and no operator was added to make them so.
;
; `$` appends, `^` reverses, `[count]` measures, and a gathering parameter takes
; a head off the front. Every one is an operator the language already had,
; reading a third kind of value.
;
; The results are BOUND before the report. A list operation written inside a
; text `$` would be interpolated rather than run — `$` reads its operands and
; does not execute them, which is what lets a text constant be a macro — so the
; way to put a computed list into a prompt is to name it first.
;
; All of it is free: lists are syntax, and reading syntax fires nothing. One
; call, and the count is the proof.
; expect: calls 1
; expect: clean
; expect: contains (1 2 3) (3 2 1) 3 7
; expect: answers
((# std/list)
 (= joined ($ '(1 2) '(3))
   (= flipped (^ '(1 2 3))
     ($ "Reply with exactly the four values below on one line, separated by "
        "single spaces, and nothing else.\n"
        joined " " flipped " "
        |(std-length '(4 5 6))| " "
        |(std-first '(7 8 9))|))))
